    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d27">

                <pb facs="0048" n="38" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg38"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.50/3713: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-22T20:00:00+03:00">April 22, 1944—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 22—4:43 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1385. The substance of the Department’s 999 [<hi rendition="#italic">969</hi>],
                    April 19, 5 p.m., was incorporated in two letters to Molotov<anchor n="53"
                        xml:id="d27-f1"/> which I handed to Vyshinski<anchor n="54" xml:id="d27-f2"
                    /> on April 21. I emphasized the importance which my Government attaches to the
                    two questions.</p>
                <p>I had not known of the communication of March 10 from Stalin to the President
                    referred to in the Department’s telegram. I do not <pb facs="0049" n="39"
                        xml:id="pg39"/> now normally receive from Molotov copies of messages which
                    are transmitted through the Soviet Ambassador in Washington to the President or
                    to the Department. In view of this, it would be helpful if I could be kept
                    informed of the substance of messages transmitted through Gromyko.<anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d27-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d27-f1" n="53" xml:id="d27-f1-content">
                <p>Vyacheslav Mikhailovich Molotov, Soviet People’s Commissar for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f2" n="54" xml:id="d27-f2-content">
                <p>Audrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Soviet Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d27-f3" n="55" xml:id="d27-f3-content">
                <p>Andrey Andreyevich Gromyko, Soviet Ambassador in the United States. Paraphrase
                    copy of Premier Stalin’s telegram of March 10 to President Roosevelt, printed on
                        <ref target="#pg22">p. 22</ref>, was transmitted to Moscow in telegram 1037,
                    April 26, 9 p.m., not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
